---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}
const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find((c) => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      SQL formatting rewrites a one-line or messy query with uppercase keywords, one major clause per
      line, and indented lists inside parentheses. The data in string literals and the spelling of
      identifiers stay the same. Formatting runs in your browser, so the query is never uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try the SQL Formatter →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The page tokenises your SQL into words, strings, comments, numbers and punctuation. Keywords from
      everyday Postgres, MySQL and SQLite are uppercased. SELECT, FROM, WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY each
      start a new line. A comma breaks a list onto the next line, and parentheses push the indent one
      level deeper. Therefore a one-line log paste becomes a readable block without changing what the
      database would run.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="dialects">
    <h2>Which SQL Dialects Are Supported? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Everyday Postgres, MySQL and SQLite keywords are recognised: SELECT, JOIN variants, GROUP BY,
      WITH, RETURNING and dollar-quoted strings. Therefore a format sql pass on a typical app query
      uppercases the right words. Exotic vendor extensions stay as ordinary identifiers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sql-formatter-example">
    <h2>SQL Formatter Example <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      For example, paste
      <code>select u.id, u.name from users u join orders o on o.user_id = u.id where o.total &gt; 100 order by o.created_at desc</code>.
      The output puts SELECT, FROM, JOIN, WHERE and ORDER BY on their own lines, keeps the identifiers
      as typed, and leaves the comparison intact. That is the same query, only easier to review.
      How to pretty-print SQL with joins is the same path: paste the long JOIN, read each ON clause
      on its own indent, and use the result in a code review.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="pretty-print">
    <h2>Pretty Print SQL and Beautify SQL <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use this page as a sql beautifier or sql pretty print pass when a one-line query is hard to read.
      Whitespace and keyword case are insignificant to the database. A formatter uses that freedom to
      make structure visible. Comments that start with <code>--</code> or sit between <code>/*</code>
      and <code>*/</code> are kept on their own lines, however, so a note that explains a tricky
      predicate is never deleted. Beautify sql and pretty print sql mean the same layout job here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="vs-alternatives">
    <h2>SQL Formatter vs Alternatives <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An IDE formatter needs a project open and a dialect plugin. SQL formatter vs uploading to a web
      service is the privacy question: many online formatters post your text to a server so they can
      run a library there. This page is the middle path: open it, paste, and read the result with no
      account and no upload. It does not rewrite or optimise the query plan. However, when you need
      the JSON the query returns formatted too, open the JSON Formatter next. Therefore you stay in
      the browser for both the SQL and the payload.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Why a Browser-Only Formatter Matters <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Production SQL sometimes still contains a password, token or connection string. Any formatter
      that uploads your text can leave that secret in a log. This page runs entirely in your browser.
      If a quoted value looks like a password or API key, you also get a warning so it is not the next
      thing you paste into a gist.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="errors">
    <h2>Unclosed Quotes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An odd number of single quotes, double quotes or backticks stops the run with a clear error. The
      tool does not guess where the string should have ended. Fix the quote and try again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Pasting a query that still contains a live password into an upload formatter is the costly mistake.
      Expecting the formatter to change the query plan is the other: layout is the only job. Making a
      long JOIN readable before a code review is the everyday win. Rotate any secret that has already
      been pasted elsewhere, then format a redacted copy here.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
